<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1594">1594</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim was a Bavarian-born field marshal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. A convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, he became famous as a bold cavalry commander, took part in the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 1631 and was mortally wounded at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> in 1632. He was the best-known member of the hereditary marshals of Pappenheim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottfried-heinrich-graf-zu-pappenheim.jpg" alt="Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gottfried_Henrich_von_Pappenheim-Treuchtlingen,_1594-1632_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15460.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim was born on 29 May 1594 in Treuchtlingen, a secondary seat of his family, the ruling lords of Pappenheim on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He was the second son of Veit zu Pappenheim and his second wife, Maria Salome von Preysing-Kopfsburg. He was educated at Altdorf and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> and travelled in southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, learning languages and seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> adventures, and in 1614 he adopted the Roman Catholic faith. When the war broke out he gave up a legal and diplomatic career and took service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, where he gained experience of cavalry fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Pappenheim soon became a lieutenant-colonel and showed great courage at the Battle of the White Mountain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 8 November 1620, where he was left for dead. In 1622 he became colonel of a cuirassier regiment, and in 1623 he served the Spaniards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Grisons</a>; his long defence of Riva on Lake Mezzola brought him to the front. In 1626 Maximilian I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> sent him to crush a peasant rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, which he did in a few weeks with ruthless severity. He then served under Tilly against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, and in 1628 he was made a count of the empire.</p><p>Pappenheim, like Tilly, has been accused of savage cruelty in the siege and storm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Breitenfeld</a> the imperial loss was not a little due to his impetuous cavalry, but he covered the retreat with skill and later campaigned between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>, delaying reinforcements for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. As a field marshal he was recalled to join Wallenstein in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and then sent towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Recalled in haste, Pappenheim arrived with his horsemen in the midst of the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> on 16 November 1632 (6 November by Swedish reckoning) and his attack was at first successful. At about the time the king was killed, he received a mortal wound elsewhere in the field and died later that day or early the next morning on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>; his body was embalmed at the Pleissenburg. A form of rapier, the Pappenheimer, is reportedly named after him, and a Polish term of the same name denotes a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> helmet. In German the phrase Ich kenne meine Pappenheimer, from Schiller's Wallenstein, now often has a negative sense, and a drinking song honouring him is sung by student associations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gottfried Heinrich of Pappenheim</b> was a leading imperial cavalry commander in the Thirty Years' War, killed at Lützen in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pappenheim">Lordship of Pappenheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1618" aria-label="Leipzig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4830 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Gottfried Heinrich Graf zu Pappenheim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5280 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5280 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.34,12.38&amp;year=1618">What was here in 1618? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
